    "result_text": "The build passes. The single acceptance test (`coreRejectsDuplicateEmail`) is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the duplicate-email rejection by following the existing telephone-uniqueness convention through each layer:\n\n1. **`OwnerRepository`** \u2014 added `existsByEmailIgnoreCase(String email)`, documented as a case-insensitive (lower-cased) email existence check.\n\n2. **Three repository implementations**, each mirroring its `existsByTelephone` counterpart but comparing case-insensitively:\n   - `SpringDataOwnerRepository` \u2014 derived query `existsByEmailIgnoreCase` (Spring Data's `IgnoreCase` keyword handles the lower-casing).\n   - `JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl` \u2014 JPQL `WHERE LOWER(owner.email) = LOWER(:email)`.\n   - `JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl` \u2014 SQL `WHERE LOWER(email) = LOWER(:email)`.\n\n3. **`ClinicService` / `ClinicServiceImpl`** \u2014 added `existsOwnerByEmail(String)` as a `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` facade delegating to the repository.\n\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 added a conflict check alongside the existing ones, reusing the already-normalized (trimmed + lower-cased) `email` value and guarding for `null` since email is optional:\n   ```java\n   if (email != null && clinicService.existsOwnerByEmail(email)) {\n       return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.CONFLICT);\n   }\n   ```\n\nEach piece sits in its proper layer, reuses the existing normalization and the established `existsBy\u2026` pattern, and no class accumulated unrelated logic.",
